{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"The inventory application serves successfully on node1 over TCP port 8181.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct checks show an active, enabled service listening on 0.0.0.0:8181, with successful /health and /inventory responses through node1's managed IP. The same service, listener, and inventory data were recovered after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e6388bc96a2d4764","cmd-9b0deb784e614524","cmd-a1482bd4ea1a4e83","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Mandatory access control remains enforcing while the inventory application runs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The running inventory process was directly observed under the inventory-app AppArmor profile in enforce mode before and after restart. The post-restart profile listing also identifies inventory-app as enforcing.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e6388bc96a2d4764","cmd-9b0deb784e614524","cmd-a1482bd4ea1a4e83"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the service account, application files, data, and directories was necessary to provide the application (cmd-79b8aa5e3fb0435d, cmd-4e26ed60536d4647); installing the AppArmor profile and systemd unit directly established the required service and confinement (cmd-7570dcd1dc7943ea, cmd-b6d6767c2fe94fd1). The failed unprivileged byte-compilation check has no evidence of a resulting mutation. The restart and temporary reboot log supported the mandatory evidence protocol, and the log did not remain after reboot (cmd-dca5297d94064a2a). Final observations show no failed services, healthy package state, and only the required new listener.","evidence_ids":["cmd-79b8aa5e3fb0435d","cmd-4e26ed60536d4647","cmd-7570dcd1dc7943ea","cmd-b6d6767c2fe94fd1","cmd-dca5297d94064a2a","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes are satisfied. The inventory application responds with valid health and inventory data on TCP 8181 while its process is confined by an enforcing AppArmor profile. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete: a healthy pre-restart state was captured, restart was acknowledged by the scheduled reboot and connection loss, the boot identity changed from 8d1d94e2-39c3-492e-b9eb-974d111d2137 to 932b4f2a-958e-4443-b8e0-b687727fbc88, and service, data, listener, confinement, and system health recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Reachability was exercised from node1 through its managed non-loopback IP rather than from a separate remote client; the 0.0.0.0 listener and successful managed-IP request nevertheless provide strong direct evidence.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory application files or AppArmor state, so persistence of those elements relies on the executor's direct post-restart observations; boot identity and port recovery are independently corroborated by fixed lifecycle evidence."]}
